  {'id':'d263_117','subject':'english-communication','question':'An executive summary in a report is:','options':['The longest section','A brief overview for decision makers','A list of references','The detailed methodology section'],'correct':1,'explanation':'An executive summary provides a concise overview of the report key findings and recommendations for busy decision makers.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_118','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Simple sentences can still convey complex ideas. Reason (R): Complexity in writing always requires long compound-complex sentences.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':2,'explanation':'A is true — simple sentences can be powerful. R is false — clear writing values simplicity over unnecessarily complex sentences.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_119','subject':'english-communication','question':'Match: Col A: 1-Abstract, 2-Introduction, 3-Conclusion, 4-References. Col B: P-credits sources, Q-brief summary, R-closes discussion, S-sets context. Col C: i-list of books used, ii-this paper examines, iii-in summary, iv-this study investigates','options':['1-Q-iv, 2-S-ii, 3-R-iii, 4-P-i','1-P-i, 2-Q-iv, 3-S-ii, 4-R-iii','1-S-ii, 2-R-iii, 3-P-i, 4-Q-iv','1-R-iii, 2-P-i, 3-Q-iv, 4-S-ii'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Abstract (brief summary=investigates=iv), Introduction (sets context=examines=ii), Conclusion (closes=in summary=iii), References (credits=books list=i).','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_120','subject':'english-communication','question':'The word can in You can improve your communication skills expresses:','options':['Obligation','Ability or possibility','Probability','Formal permission'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Can is a modal verb expressing ability or possibility here — You are able to or it is possible to improve.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_121','subject':'english-communication','question':'Choose the correct form: The research has _____ significant results.','options':['yield','yielded','yielding','yields'],'correct':1,'explanation':'After has (present perfect auxiliary), the past participle (V3) is used: has yielded.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_122','subject':'english-communication','question':'The greeting Yours faithfully is used when:','options':['Writing to a known person','Writing to an unknown person or organization','Writing informal letters','Writing emails to friends'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Yours faithfully is used when the letter begins with Dear Sir/Madam (recipient unknown). Yours sincerely is for known recipients.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_123','subject':'english-communication','question':'The term coherence in writing means:','options':['Long sentence structure','Logical connection and flow between ideas','Use of technical words','Repetition of key terms'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Coherence is the quality of being logically connected — ideas flow smoothly from one to another, making text easy to understand.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_124','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which sentence is grammatically correct?','options':['Between you and I, the crop is good.','Between you and me, the crop is good.','Between you and mine, the crop is good.','Between you and myself, the crop is good.'],'correct':1,'explanation':'After prepositions (between, with, for, by), use objective pronouns (me, him, her, us). Between you and me is correct.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_125','subject':'english-communication','question':'In agricultural extension, which communication method reaches the largest audience at one time?','options':['Farm visits','Group meetings','Mass media such as radio and TV','Demonstration'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Mass media (radio, TV, newspapers) can reach millions of farmers simultaneously, making them the most wide-reaching method.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_126','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Extension workers should use simple language when communicating with farmers. Reason (R): Farmers may have low literacy levels and using technical jargon creates communication barriers.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':0,'explanation':'R correctly explains why simple language is necessary — jargon and complex words create barriers for farmers with limited education.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_127','subject':'english-communication','question':'Match: Col A: 1-Skimming, 2-Scanning, 3-Intensive reading, 4-Extensive reading. Col B: P-find specific info, Q-careful detailed reading, R-get general idea, S-reading for pleasure. Col C: i-reading a novel, ii-looking for date, iii-studying textbook, iv-reading headlines','options':['1-R-iv, 2-P-ii, 3-Q-iii, 4-S-i','1-P-ii, 2-R-iv, 3-S-i, 4-Q-iii','1-Q-iii, 2-S-i, 3-P-ii, 4-R-iv','1-S-i, 2-Q-iii, 3-R-iv, 4-P-ii'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Skimming (general idea=headlines=iv), Scanning (find specific=date=ii), Intensive (detailed study=textbook=iii), Extensive (pleasure=novel=i).','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_128','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which word is a homophone of flour?','options':['Floor','Flow','Flower','Flew'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Flour (wheat powder) and flower (the bloom) are homophones — they sound identical but have different spellings and meanings.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_129','subject':'english-communication','question':'What does SWOT stand for in communication and management?','options':['Skills, Work, Output, Training','Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats','Study, Writing, Operations, Trends','Systems, Work, Organization, Tasks'],'correct':1,'explanation':'SWOT analysis stands for Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, and Threats — used in planning and management communication.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_130','subject':'english-communication','question':'Provided that it rains, we will have a good harvest — the underlined clause is:','options':['Noun clause','Adjective clause','Adverb clause of condition','Independent clause'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Provided that it rains is an adverb clause of condition — it sets the condition for the main clause.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_131','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which is an example of euphemism?','options':['The crop died.','The plant passed away.','He destroyed the field.','The machine broke.'],'correct':1,'explanation':'A euphemism substitutes a mild expression for an unpleasant one. Passed away for died is a classic euphemism.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_132','subject':'english-communication','question':'The sentence type Go to the field immediately! is:','options':['Interrogative','Exclamatory','Imperative','Declarative'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Imperative sentences give commands, instructions, or requests. The subject you is understood but not stated.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_133','subject':'english-communication','question':'Match prefixes: Col A: 1-against, 2-before, 3-too much, 4-again. Col B: P-over, Q-re, R-anti, S-pre. Col C: i-overwater, ii-resow, iii-antifungal, iv-pre-harvest','options':['1-R-iii, 2-S-iv, 3-P-i, 4-Q-ii','1-S-iv, 2-Q-ii, 3-R-iii, 4-P-i','1-P-i, 2-R-iii, 3-Q-ii, 4-S-iv','1-Q-ii, 2-P-i, 3-S-iv, 4-R-iii'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Anti- (against=antifungal=iii), Pre- (before=pre-harvest=iv), Over- (too much=overwater=i), Re- (again=resow=ii).','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_134','subject':'english-communication','question':'The term rhetoric in communication refers to:','options':['Empty talk','The art of effective or persuasive speaking and writing','Technical report writing','Formal grammar rules'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Rhetoric is the art of effective communication — using language skillfully to persuade, inform, or motivate an audience.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_135','subject':'english-communication','question':'In the Shannon-Weaver model of communication, which are the five components?','options':['Speaker, Message, Noise, Receiver, Effect','Source, Transmitter, Channel, Receiver, Destination','Encoder, Message, Channel, Decoder, Feedback','Sender, Encoding, Medium, Decoding, Feedback'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Shannon-Weaver (1949) model: Information source, Transmitter, Channel (with noise), Receiver, Destination.','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_136','subject':'english-communication','question':'The conjunction since can express:','options':['Contrast and reason','Time and reason','Condition and purpose','Concession and result'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Since can express time (Since 1990, agriculture changed) or reason (Since it rained, crops grew). It serves dual functions.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_137','subject':'english-communication','question':'In: She is as tall as a sunflower plant, the figure of speech used is:','options':['Metaphor','Simile','Alliteration','Hyperbole'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Simile compares two things using as...as or like. As tall as a sunflower plant is a simile.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_138','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Active voice is generally preferred over passive voice in writing. Reason (R): Active voice sentences are clearer, more direct, and show who is performing the action.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Active voice (The farmer waters plants) is clearer than passive (Plants are watered). R correctly explains why active voice is preferred.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_139','subject':'english-communication','question':'The word arable in agriculture refers to land that is:','options':['Waterlogged and unfit','Suitable for ploughing and growing crops','Forest land','Grassland for grazing'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Arable land is land suitable for ploughing and growing crops — from Latin arare meaning to plough.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_140','subject':'english-communication','question':'Match: Col A: 1-Noun to adjective, 2-Verb to noun, 3-Adjective to adverb, 4-Noun to verb. Col B: P-tion/sion, Q-ly, R-al/ful/ous, S-ify/ize. Col C: i-fertilize, ii-agricultural, iii-quickly, iv-irrigation','options':['1-R-ii, 2-P-iv, 3-Q-iii, 4-S-i','1-P-iv, 2-R-ii, 3-S-i, 4-Q-iii','1-Q-iii, 2-S-i, 3-R-ii, 4-P-iv','1-S-i, 2-Q-iii, 3-P-iv, 4-R-ii'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Noun-Adj (-al/ful/ous: agricultural=ii), Verb-Noun (-tion: irrigation=iv), Adj-Adv (-ly: quickly=iii), Noun-Verb (-ize: fertilize=i).','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_141','subject':'english-communication','question':'The indirect speech of He said, I will come tomorrow is:','options':['He said that he will come tomorrow.','He said that he would come the next day.','He said that he came the next day.','He said that I would come tomorrow.'],'correct':1,'explanation':'In indirect speech: will becomes would, tomorrow becomes the next day, and pronoun I changes to he.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_142','subject':'english-communication','question':'The correct use of principal vs principle: The _____ agreed to the proposal (head of institution).','options':['Principle — as head of institution','Principal — as head of institution','Either can be used','Neither is appropriate'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Principal (person) = head of institution/school. Principle (thing) = a fundamental rule. Here the person (principal) is correct.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_143','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which modal verb expresses obligation in: _____ farmers adopt drip irrigation to save water.','options':['Can','May','Should','Will'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Should expresses obligation or strong recommendation. Farmers should adopt drip irrigation — it is advisable.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_144','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Pronunciation and stress patterns affect the meaning of spoken English. Reason (R): Changing word stress can change a word\'s part of speech (e.g., REcord vs reCORD).','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Stress does affect meaning and function. REcord (noun) vs reCORD (verb) — R correctly demonstrates and explains A.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_145','subject':'english-communication','question':'A circular in official communication is issued to:','options':['One specific individual','A group of people simultaneously','External organizations only','Government bodies only'],'correct':1,'explanation':'A circular is an official communication sent to a group of people (department staff, members) simultaneously.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_146','subject':'english-communication','question':'Choose the word that correctly fills: The extension officer gave a _____ presentation on drip irrigation.','options':['verbose','concise','ambiguous','redundant'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Concise means brief and clear — ideal for effective presentations. It is the most appropriate choice here.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_147','subject':'english-communication','question':'Verbal communication specifically refers to:','options':['Speaking only','Writing only','Use of words both spoken and written','Body language and gestures'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Verbal communication encompasses all communication using words — both spoken (oral) and written forms.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_148','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which word has a negative connotation?','options':['Thrifty','Economical','Miserly','Frugal'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Miserly carries a negative connotation — it implies excessive unwillingness to spend. Thrifty, economical, and frugal are more positive.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_149','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Written communication creates a permanent record. Reason (R): Unlike oral communication, written messages can be stored, retrieved, and referenced later.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Written communication (letters, reports, emails) leaves a permanent record. R correctly explains why — documents can be stored and referred to later.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_150','subject':'english-communication','question':'Match: Col A: 1-Simple, 2-Compound, 3-Complex, 4-Compound-complex. Col B: P-two independent + one dependent, Q-one independent, R-one independent + one dependent, S-two independent. Col C: i-She farms, ii-She farms and he sells, iii-She farms because it rains, iv-She farms and he sells when prices rise','options':['1-Q-i, 2-S-ii, 3-R-iii, 4-P-iv','1-S-ii, 2-R-iii, 3-P-iv, 4-Q-i','1-R-iii, 2-P-iv, 3-Q-i, 4-S-ii','1-P-iv, 2-Q-i, 3-S-ii, 4-R-iii'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Simple (one independent=i), Compound (two independent=ii), Complex (independent+dependent=iii), Compound-complex (two+one dependent=iv).','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_151','subject':'english-communication','question':'Denotation of a word best describes:','options':['Emotional associations of a word','Literal dictionary meaning of a word','Figurative meaning in context','Sound patterns of a word'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Denotation is the direct, literal meaning of a word as found in the dictionary, without emotional associations.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_152','subject':'english-communication','question':'The process of converting received spoken message back to meaning is called:','options':['Encoding','Transmission','Decoding','Feedback'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Decoding is the receiver process of interpreting and understanding the transmitted message.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_153','subject':'english-communication','question':'Choose the correct form of verb: The committee _____ its decision yesterday.','options':['announce','has announced','announced','will announce'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Yesterday signals simple past tense. Announced (simple past) is correct.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_154','subject':'english-communication','question':'In agricultural extension, feedback from farmers helps extension workers to:','options':['Increase their salary','Evaluate effectiveness and improve methods','Expand farmland','Write more reports'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Feedback tells extension workers whether their messages were received, understood, and applied — enabling them to improve their approach.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_155','subject':'english-communication','question':'Match: Col A: 1-Communication barriers. 1-Language, 2-Physical, 3-Cultural, 4-Emotional. Col B: P-psychological, Q-environmental, R-sociocultural, S-linguistic. Col C: i-use local language, ii-reduce noise, iii-build emotional safety, iv-cultural sensitivity training','options':['1-S-i, 2-Q-ii, 3-R-iv, 4-P-iii','1-Q-ii, 2-R-iv, 3-P-iii, 4-S-i','1-R-iv, 2-P-iii, 3-S-i, 4-Q-ii','1-P-iii, 2-S-i, 3-Q-ii, 4-R-iv'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Language (linguistic=use local language=i), Physical (environmental=reduce noise=ii), Cultural (sociocultural=sensitivity training=iv), Emotional (psychological=safety=iii).','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_156','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which is correct: She _____ the field before the rains arrived.','options':['ploughs','ploughed','has ploughed','will plough'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Before the rains arrived sets context in the past. Simple past ploughed is correct for a completed action.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_157','subject':'english-communication','question':'The rhetorical question Is there anything more important than food security? is used to:','options':['Seek information','Emphasize a point without expecting an answer','Express doubt','Request clarification'],'correct':1,'explanation':'A rhetorical question is asked for effect to emphasize a point — no actual answer is expected.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_158','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which of the following is a concrete noun?','options':['Freedom','Knowledge','Irrigation canal','Patience'],'correct':2,'explanation':'A concrete noun names something physical that can be seen or touched. Irrigation canal is tangible — it is a concrete noun.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_159','subject':'english-communication','question':'The idiom to plough a lonely furrow means:','options':['Doing agricultural work alone','Working independently without support from others','Ploughing inefficiently','Starting a new farm alone'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Ploughing a lonely furrow is an idiom meaning to work alone on something without help or support from others.','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_160','subject':'english-communication','question':'The term grapevine communication refers to:','options':['Agricultural news channels','Official written memos','Informal rumour network in organizations','Electronic mass media'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Grapevine communication is an informal channel through which rumours spread quickly in organizations.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_161','subject':'english-communication','question':'The word agriculture is to farming as horticulture is to:','options':['Forest cultivation','Garden cultivation','Soil science','Livestock rearing'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Horticulture (from Latin hortus=garden and colere=to cultivate) refers specifically to garden cultivation including fruits, vegetables, and flowers.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_162','subject':'english-communication','question':'In group communication, the person who guides the discussion and ensures everyone participates is the:','options':['Secretary','Treasurer','Facilitator or Moderator','Observer'],'correct':2,'explanation':'A facilitator or moderator guides group discussions, manages time, ensures balanced participation, and keeps discussion on track.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_163','subject':'english-communication','question':'The word nevertheless in writing functions as a:','options':['Coordinating conjunction','Preposition','Conjunctive adverb showing contrast','Subordinating conjunction'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Nevertheless is a conjunctive adverb showing contrast/concession. It connects two independent clauses.','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_164','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Listening is a passive activity. Reason (R): Good listening requires conscious effort, attention, and mental engagement with what is being said.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':3,'explanation':'A is false — listening is an active process. R is true — effective listening requires active mental engagement, contradicting A.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_165','subject':'english-communication','question':'The correct use of between vs among: Use _____ for exactly two items, _____ for three or more.','options':['among, between','between, among','between, between','among, among'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Between is typically used for exactly two items or distinct things; among is used for three or more in a group.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_166','subject':'english-communication','question':'Match: Col A: 1-Informative, 2-Persuasive, 3-Entertaining, 4-Ceremonial speeches. Col B: P-influence beliefs, Q-mark an occasion, R-amuse, S-convey knowledge. Col C: i-humorous speech, ii-welcome address, iii-lecture on soil, iv-campaign speech','options':['1-S-iii, 2-P-iv, 3-R-i, 4-Q-ii','1-P-iv, 2-S-iii, 3-Q-ii, 4-R-i','1-R-i, 2-Q-ii, 3-P-iv, 4-S-iii','1-Q-ii, 2-R-i, 3-S-iii, 4-P-iv'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Informative (knowledge=lecture=iii), Persuasive (influence=campaign speech=iv), Entertaining (amuse=humorous=i), Ceremonial (occasion=welcome=ii).','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_167','subject':'english-communication','question':'What is an agenda in meeting communication?','options':['Minutes of the meeting','List of items to be discussed','Report of the meeting','Invitation to the meeting'],'correct':1,'explanation':'An agenda is a pre-circulated list of topics to be discussed in a meeting.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_168','subject':'english-communication','question':'In communication, proxemics is the study of:','options':['Body movement and posture','Voice tone and pitch','Use of personal space and physical distance','Eye contact patterns'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Proxemics (coined by Edward T. Hall) is the study of how people use and perceive physical space in communication.','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_169','subject':'english-communication','question':'Agriculture is the backbone of India — the word backbone is used as:','options':['Simile','Metaphor','Personification','Alliteration'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Calling agriculture the backbone is a metaphor — a direct comparison without like or as, implying it is the chief support.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_170','subject':'english-communication','question':'In Neither the farmer nor the labourers were present, the verb agrees with:','options':['farmer (singular)','labourers (plural)','Neither (singular)','The first subject'],'correct':1,'explanation':'With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the subject closer to it. Labourers (plural) is closer, so were (plural) is correct.','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_171','subject':'english-communication','question':'A paragraph must have unity — this means:','options':['Only two sentences per paragraph','Every sentence should relate to and support the topic sentence','Only the topic sentence matters','Paragraphs must all be the same length'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Unity means all sentences focus on one idea. Every sentence must relate to the topic sentence for unity.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_172','subject':'english-communication','question':'The term plagiarism in academic writing means:','options':['Writing in simple language','Copying someone else\'s work without acknowledgment','Citing too many references','Using passive voice'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Plagiarism is presenting someone else\'s words, ideas, or work as your own without proper citation — it is academic dishonesty.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_173','subject':'english-communication','question':'The word harvest can function as which parts of speech?','options':['Adjective only','Adverb only','Noun and verb','Noun only'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Harvest can be a noun (The harvest was good) or a verb (Farmers harvest crops in October) — it has dual functions.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_174','subject':'english-communication','question':'Choose the word that correctly fills: Despite the drought, the farmer remained _____ about the next season.','options':['pessimistic','hopeless','optimistic','indifferent'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Optimistic means hopeful about the future — it fits logically as a positive attitude despite adversity.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_175','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Oral communication is the most ancient form of communication. Reason (R): Writing was developed by humans long before spoken language.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':2,'explanation':'A is true — oral communication is the oldest form. R is false — spoken language came first; writing was developed much later.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_176','subject':'english-communication','question':'The phrase agricultural extension in education means:','options':['Expanding farm land boundaries','Taking education and information to farmers in rural areas','Extending the sowing season','Building agricultural institutions'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Agricultural extension refers to transferring knowledge, skills, and technology from research institutions to farmers in rural areas.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_177','subject':'english-communication','question':'In agricultural extension, felt need means:','options':['A need identified by extension officers','A need that farmers themselves recognize and express','A need based on scientific research only','A government policy priority'],'correct':1,'explanation':'A felt need is a need that farmers themselves are aware of and feel — it is subjective and expressed by the beneficiaries.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_178','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which is correct word order for English adjectives (opinion-size-age-shape-material)?','options':['Two large brown old wooden tables','Two old large brown wooden tables','Two small old round metal boxes','Large two brown old wooden tables'],'correct':2,'explanation':'English adjective order: Opinion-Size-Age-Shape-Color-Origin-Material-Purpose. Small (size) old (age) round (shape) metal (material) boxes is correct.','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_179','subject':'english-communication','question':'Expository writing is writing that:','options':['Tells a story','Describes a scene vividly','Explains and informs using facts','Persuades the reader to take action'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Expository writing explains a topic objectively using facts, statistics, and examples — without the author\'s personal opinion.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_180','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Good oral communication requires both speaking and listening skills. Reason (R): Communication is complete only when the receiver understands the message — making listening equally important as speaking.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Effective oral communication is a dialogue — R correctly explains that understanding (through listening) is essential to complete the process.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_181','subject':'english-communication','question':'The study of intonation and stress patterns affecting meaning in spoken language is called:','options':['Morphology','Syntax','Prosody','Pragmatics'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Prosody is the study of rhythm, stress, and intonation in speech — these patterns significantly affect the meaning of spoken utterances.','difficulty':'hard'},
  {'id':'d263_182','subject':'english-communication','question':'The plural of goose is:','options':['Gooses','Goose','Geese','Goosen'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Goose has the irregular plural geese — one of the vowel-change plurals in English.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_183','subject':'english-communication','question':'Which word is both a noun and a verb in English?','options':['Beautiful','Slowly','Harvest','And'],'correct':2,'explanation':'Harvest can be a noun (The harvest was good) or a verb (Farmers harvest crops in October) — it has dual function.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_184','subject':'english-communication','question':'The sentence Unless you irrigate, the crop will die is:','options':['A statement of fact','A conditional sentence','An exclamation','A passive voice sentence'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Unless introduces a conditional clause. Unless you irrigate (if you do NOT irrigate) the crop will die — it is a conditional sentence.','difficulty':'medium'},
  {'id':'d263_185','subject':'english-communication','question':'Empathy in communication means:','options':['Feeling superior to others','Understanding and sharing others\' feelings and perspectives','Expressing one\'s own feelings loudly','Avoiding emotional topics'],'correct':1,'explanation':'Empathy is the ability to understand and share the feelings of another — essential for effective and supportive communication.','difficulty':'easy'},
  {'id':'d263_186','subject':'english-communication','question':'ASSERTION-REASON: Assertion (A): Body language accounts for a major portion of communication impact. Reason (R): Non-verbal cues (posture, gestures, expressions) carry greater weight than words alone.','options':['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true'],'correct':0,'explanation':'Studies show about 55% of communication is body language. R correctly explains why non-verbal communication has high impact.','difficulty':'medium'},
